{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The analogy provided in the mechanism section fundamentally undermines the target's own claim that the preference is defensible on Popper's terms. Popper explicitly rejected the rationality of acting on past success as a guide to future action, labeling such reliance as inductive. By framing the choice as analogous to a gambler using a 'track record' because it is the 'only rational move,' the target implicitly concedes that without an inductive assumption (that the track record matters), there is no rational basis for the preference. This contradicts the core claim that the preference is a non-inductive methodological rule; if the rationale requires the agent to treat past survival as a reason for future expectation (even under the guise of 'risk minimization' based on severity), it smuggles in the very principle of uniformity or predictive reliability that Popper sought to eliminate. The target fails to explain how 'minimizing risk' is calculable without assuming that a theory which has not failed yet is less likely to fail next time than an untested one, which is a probabilistic/inductive judgment Popper forbade.","premise":"The target's defense relies on an analogy between Popperian theory choice and a gambler selecting a horse based on past performance, claiming this avoids induction.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"72aab475b6473111a8cd9dcd","quote":"Popper's methodology is akin to a gambler choosing the horse with the best track record not because it will win (induction), but because it is the only rational move available given the lack of other information."}],"successor_question":"If the gambler cannot assume the track record predicts the future race, on what non-inductive grounds does Popper deem it the 'only rational move' rather than an arbitrary guess?","target":"be159e24c526e76ce015bc59ac104a876b41441c9625d02e772cb99429bfc566"}]}